UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 2: Aural skills and listening', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Pitch direction tells you whether the next note moves up, moves down, or stays the same as the note you just heard. You already know that a hum has a pitch. Today you compare neighbouring pitches instead of only naming one note as high or low. Same means you cannot hear a change. Up means the new note is higher. Down means it is lower. Letter names are not required. Your ear and a pencil are enough if you describe the three notes in order. Direction matters because a tune is a path, not a pile of separate sounds.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA friend hums a high note, then a lower note, then a note that is lower again. How should you name the two pitch movements between those three notes?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer compares each note only with the one just heard and writes down, then down. The first move leaves the high note for a lower one, so it is downward. The second move leaves that note for a still lower one, so it is downward again. Calling the whole line \"low\" hides the two steps a singer would need.\n\nWATCH FOR\nNotes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher. That fails because same means no audible change at all. A small lift is still up. If you label a slight rise as same, the path you write cannot be hummed back. Test it: hum both notes and keep the word up whenever the second note climbs.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nHum a low note, hum that same low note again, then jump to a much higher note. Write the two directions in order and say which pair stayed still. This path is not the middle-up-same example and not the high-lower-lower practice. Your page should show same, then up.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Music","learning_intention":"Pitch direction tells you whether the next note moves up, moves down, or stays the same as the note you just heard. You already know that a hum has a pitch. Today you compare neighbouring pitches instead of only naming one note as high or low. Same means you cannot hear a change. Up means the new note is higher. Down means it is lower. Letter names are not required. Your ear and a pencil are enough if you describe the three notes in order. Direction matters because a tune is a path, not a pile of separate sounds.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MU4-LIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Hum a low note, hum that same low note again, then jump to a much higher note. Write the two directions in order and say which pair stayed still. This path is not the middle-up-same example and not the high-lower-lower p Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using direction on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher.. Correction: A strong answer compares each note only with the one just heard and writes down, then down.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher. That fails because same means no audible change at all. A small lift is still up. If you label a slight rise as same, the path you write cannot be hummed back. Test it: hum both notes and keep the word up whenever the second note climbs. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating direction on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with direction, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about direction with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for direction. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for direction. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work","A strong answer compares each note only with the one just heard and writes down, then down","Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher"],"question":"A friend hums a high note, then a lower note, then a note that is lower again. How should you name the two pitch movements between those three notes?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for direction; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Pitch direction tells you whether the next note moves up, moves down, or stays the same as the note you just heard","A strong answer compares each note only with the one just heard and writes down, then down","Careful use of upward with clear evidence","Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with direction?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named upward, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply direction to new material and name the upward used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher","Name upward only, without linking it to direction"],"question":"Which response best applies direction with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on direction. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher","A strong answer compares each note only with the one just heard and writes down, then down","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing direction?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for direction"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about direction?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Pitch direction tells you whether the next note moves up, moves down) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Three hummed notes in words: start on a comfortable middle hum, slide to a clearly higher hum, then hold that same"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about direction?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for direction?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher","Name upward only, with no linked example","Hum a low note, hum that same low note again, then jump to a much higher note","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies direction to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about direction with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating direction on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using direction on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for direction. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with direction, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher.. Correction: A strong answer compares each note only with the one just heard and writes down, then down.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-music-y9-w01-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Music 7–10 (2024) outcome MU4-LIS-01. Content-point mapping is pending. Not NESA verified. No copyrighted recordings are required; examples are original descriptions or student-made sounds.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise direction.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use direction with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify upward and explain why it matters.","I can practise downward independently on a new prompt.","I can notice repeat needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Pitch direction tells you whether the next note moves up, moves down, or stays the same as the note you just heard.","options":["Pitch direction tells you whether the next note moves up, moves down, or stays the same as the note you just heard","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about direction?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThree hummed notes in words: start on a comfortable middle hum, slide to a clearly higher hum, then hold that same higher hum again. From the first note to the second, the direction is up. From the second note to the third, the direction is same, because nothing moved. Do not call the third note \"up\" just because it is still high. Compare it only with the note before it. Say the path aloud as \"up, then same\" and point to which pair each word describes.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA friend hums a high note, then a lower note, then a note that is lower again. How should you name the two pitch movements between those three notes?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer compares each note only with the one just heard and writes down, then down. The first move leaves the high note for a lower one, so it is downward. The second move leaves that note for a still lower one, so it is downward again. Calling the whole line \"low\" hides the two steps a singer would need.","explain":"Pitch direction tells you whether the next note moves up, moves down, or stays the same as the note you just heard. You already know that a hum has a pitch. Today you compare neighbouring pitches instead of only naming one note as high or low. Same means you cannot hear a change. Up means the new note is higher. Down means it is lower. Letter names are not required. Your ear and a pencil are enough if you describe the three notes in order. Direction matters because a tune is a path, not a pile of separate sounds.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher. That fails because same means no audible change at all. A small lift is still up. If you label a slight rise as same, the path you write cannot be hummed back. Test it: hum both notes and keep the word up whenever the second note climbs. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes show an upward hum change beside a downward hum change.","caption":"Direction compares a note with the one before it.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Up</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">The next hum is higher</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Down</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">The next hum is lower</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Three hummed notes in words: start on a comfortable middle hum, slide to a clearly higher hum, then hold that same highe Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Three hummed notes in words: start on a comfortable middle hum, slide to a clearly higher hum, then hold that same highe Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about direction?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThree hummed notes in words: start on a comfortable middle hum, slide to a clearly higher hum, then hold that same higher hum again. From the first note to the second, the direction is up. From the second note to the third, the direction is same, because nothing moved. Do not call the third note \"up\" just because it is still high. Compare it only with the note before it. Say the path aloud as \"up, then same\" and point to which pair each word describes. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a repeated pitch with a three-note direction path.","caption":"Same is a direction, not a leftover high note.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Same</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">No pitch change is heard</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Path</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Up, down, or same in order</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about direction?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nNotes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher. That fails because same means no audible change at all. A small lift is still up. If you label a slight rise as same, the path you write cannot be hummed back. Test it: hum both notes and keep the word up whenever the second note climbs. Here is a more helpful example: Three hummed notes in words: start on a comfortable middle hum, slide to a clearly higher hum, then hold that same higher hum again. From the first note to the second, the direction is up. From the second note to the third, the direction is same, because nothing moved. Do not call the third note \"up\" just because it is still high. Compare it only with the note before it. Say the path aloud as \"up, then same\" and point to which pair each word describes.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer compares each note only with the one just heard and writes down, then down. The first move leaves the high note for a lower one, so it is downward. The second move leaves that note for a still lower one, so it is downward again. Calling the whole line \"low\" hides the two steps a singer would need.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Notes that feel close together must be the same pitch, even when the second note is slightly higher. That fails because same means no audible change at all. A small lift is still up. If you label a slight rise as same, the path you write cannot be hummed back. Test it: hum both notes and keep the word up whenever the second note climbs. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about direction?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPitch direction tells you whether the next note moves up, moves down, or stays the same as the note you just heard. You already know that a hum has a pitch. Today you compare neighbouring pitches instead of only naming one note as high or low. Same means you cannot hear a change. Up means the new note is higher. Down means it is lower. Letter names are not required. Your ear and a pencil are enough if you describe the three notes in order. Direction matters because a tune is a path, not a pile of separate sounds. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 2: Aural skills and listening","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Aural skills and listening","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nThree hummed notes in words: start on a comfortable middle hum, slide to a clearly higher hum, then hold that same higher hum again. From the first note to the second, the direction is up. From the second note to the third, the direction is same, because nothing moved. Do not call the third note \"up\" just because it is still high. Compare it only with the note before it. Say the path aloud as \"up, then same\" and point to which pair each word describes.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two boxes show an upward hum change beside a downward hum change.","caption":"Direction compares a note with the one before it.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Up</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">The next hum is higher</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Down</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">The next hum is lower</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a repeated pitch with a three-note direction path.","caption":"Same is a direction, not a leftover high note.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Same</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">No pitch change is heard</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Path</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Up, down, or same in order</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-music-y9-w01-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
